Yes. The interesting version is augmented-reality cinema that crosses the boundary from “watching a story” into temporarily inhabiting one.

Once the medium becomes convincing enough, the design problem changes. You are no longer only asking whether the plot works. You are asking what happens when the nervous system starts treating fictional events as socially or emotionally real enough to produce genuine grief, loyalty, fear, attachment, or memory.

That gives you a threshold model:

obvious fiction
→ immersive fiction
→ emotionally real fiction
→ behaviorally consequential fiction
→ reality-monitoring challenge

Past some point, the relevant question is not just realism. It is whether the participant can still tell:

what happened
from
what was simulated
from
what I felt
from
what I now believe

Those can diverge.

And yes, people may adapt. Humans have repeatedly learned new media grammars—stage, radio, cinema, television, games, VR. The concern is that an adaptive AI-driven medium could move faster than our learned defenses because it can personalize the illusion to the individual rather than broadcasting the same artifact to everyone.

That is where consent has to become structural, not cosmetic.

The powerful version is:

high immersion + explicit entry + reversible exit + provenance + participant control

not:

high immersion + hidden manipulation + “they seemed willing enough”

Especially once you bring in neurotechnology, biometrics, animal interfaces, or individualized psychological modeling. “Willingly” needs to mean they actually understand the relevant interaction and can stop it—not merely that the system found a framing that made refusal less likely.

Your cross-species point is still fertile, though. Comparative biology gives you different sensory worlds:

human visual/social world
dog olfactory world
bat acoustic world
bird magnetic/polarization cues
fish electro/mechanosensory world

Technology could translate one perceptual ecology into another—not literally giving us the animal’s subjective experience, but giving us a structured approximation of the variables available to it.

So an AR “movie” could eventually be less:

watch what the wolf does

and more:

temporarily inhabit a model of what information the wolf can perceive.

That is a much more interesting medium.

And the same goes for people. Not “overwrite their reality,” but let them consensually enter another representational world, learn its grammar, then come back with something they did not have before.

That would make the medium closer to:

cinema + simulation + ritual + education + game + sensory prosthesis

The strongest principle I’d keep is the one you ended on:

make participation genuinely attractive enough that coercion is unnecessary.

If the experience is worth entering, people will choose the door themselves.